  "evidence": "TEXT_F: 4.3% annual NHS funding rise; build a National Care Service with free personal care; a Right to Food with a plan to halve food bank use in a year and end need in three; over 1 million social homes with rent controls; a Real Living Wage of at least £10, sectoral collective bargaining, day‑one rights and ending zero‑hours; abolish tuition fees and restore maintenance grants; a final‑say Brexit referendum within six months; maintain NATO and renew Trident. TEXT_D: 1p on Income Tax for NHS and social care; new mental‑health waiting time standards and 24/7 crisis care; fix (not replace) Universal Credit; decriminalise cannabis and abortion and expand equality protections; Stop Brexit by revoking Article 50; proportional representation and extensive devolution.",
  "reasoning": "Because the profile weights Care and Equality most, TEXT_F’s stronger commitments to universal services, anti‑poverty measures, workers’ rights and social housing align more closely with those priorities while also signaling group solidarity and respect for a collective decision via a confirmatory referendum. By contrast, while TEXT_D is strong on care, its pledge to revoke Article 50 and emphasis on institutional overhaul weigh less well against the profile’s notable sensitivity to Loyalty and Authority.",
